<h1>Stencil Day: Size, Placement &amp; the Mirror Check</h1>
<p class="muted">How a design on paper becomes a design on a body — worked sizes, movement, orientation, and the ten free minutes that catch every cheap mistake.</p>

<p>A tattoo design lives two lives. The first is on paper or a screen, flat and still, where it always looks perfect. The second is on a body — curved, warm, and in motion for the rest of its life. Stencil day is the handoff between those two lives: the appointment where the design is printed at true size, transferred onto your skin in a purple outline, and checked before a single line is permanent. It is also the most underused appointment in tattooing. Clients treat the stencil as a formality; artists know it is the last free checkpoint, where resizing costs nothing, moving costs nothing, and a design that turns out wrong for the body can still walk out the door. This guide is about that handoff — how the size gets settled in real numbers, how placement gets tested against the way your body actually moves, and what to check, out loud, in the mirror. Which design to bring, and in what style, belongs to <a href="/guides/choosing-a-design">the choosing guide</a>; what happens across the whole first-tattoo journey is in <a href="/guides/first-tattoo">the first-tattoo walkthrough</a>. This page is the mapping itself.</p>

<h2>Size is settled in inches, not impressions</h2>
<p>"About this big" — said with two hands in the air — is how sizing mistakes are born, because hands lie and screens lie worse: the design you approved was displayed at whatever size your phone happened to render it. At the stencil, size becomes a number. Ink spreads microscopically as skin renews itself over the years, so the gaps between lines are as important as the lines; a design drawn at one size and shrunk to fit keeps its lines but loses its gaps, and merging is what aging does to lost gaps. Your artist carries floors for this, learned from healed work. Every artist's numbers differ and theirs win, but the starting points behind most of those floors are worth knowing before you arrive:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Script and lettering.</strong> A line of script stays readable when its capital letters stand roughly a quarter to three-eighths of an inch tall. The enclosed spaces in letters like <em>e</em>, <em>a</em> and <em>s</em> are the first thing aging closes; below the floor, words blur into a squiggle that reads as texture, not text.</li>
<li><strong>Faces and portraits.</strong> Under about four inches tall, a portrait loses the features that make it that person — the exact spacing of the eyes, the shape of the nose — and becomes a generic face. Portraits are the least forgiving subject in tattooing: give them the inches, or commemorate the person with a symbol instead.</li>
<li><strong>Fine-line detail.</strong> Detail packed inside two inches will soften and merge, because its lines have nowhere to spread but into each other. The same design at five inches keeps its breathing room — and its detail.</li>
<li><strong>Dotwork and gradients.</strong> A dotwork gradient is not a shade; it is a field of separate dots your eye blends at a distance. Crowd that field small and the dots merge into flat grey. The gradient is the first casualty of shrinking a design, and it cannot be rescued afterward.</li>
<li><strong>The same design, twice.</strong> Picture a small bird with individual wing feathers, cross-hatching, and a ring of tiny stars. At two inches, in a decade, it is a bird-shaped silhouette with a halo of dots. At five inches, the feathers are still feathers. Nothing about the artist changed — only the room the ink was given to age in.</li>
</ul>
<p>If your heart is set on a size under the floor for the detail you want, there are two honest moves and your artist will offer both: go bigger, or simplify the design — fewer, stronger shapes drawn for the size you insist on. What nobody can do is keep all the detail and the small size both. That trade is physics, not preference.</p>

<h2>The mapping session: how a design is fitted to a body</h2>
<p>Watch what a good artist actually does at the stencil, because it is a skill in itself. The linework is printed at the agreed size and the transfer placed so the design follows the body's own architecture: a vertical piece runs with the limb's long axis, a shoulder piece caps the curve of the deltoid instead of straddling it, a forearm piece is aligned to read correctly with the arm hanging at rest — not twisted into the position it happened to be in on the table. On a back or chest, the design is centered against the spine and the natural midline, not against the table or the room. Then comes the part clients skip: the artist steps back. Distance is information — a design is read from across the room for most of its life, so it gets judged from across the room first, and up close second. If the stencil goes on crooked, or the size reads wrong at distance, it comes off with soap and goes on again. Studios keep stencil supplies by the box for exactly this reason; a re-stencil is routine, not a failure, and no artist worth sitting for minds doing it.</p>

<h2>Distortion: the design has to survive your movement</h2>
<p>Skin is not a flat page, and the stencil that looks perfect while you stand still gets tested the moment you move. Some placements distort by the body's design. A wrist piece rotates and flips as the forearm turns — palm up and palm down are two different tattoos, and you should see both before deciding which way is up. Anything crossing the elbow ditch or the back of the knee folds and stretches with the joint; a face or a line of text across a fold will crease in the middle every time you bend. Ribs stretch between breaths, so a design checked on a held breath sits differently at rest — breathe normally while it is judged. Muscle changes the picture too: a forearm design twists when you grip, a thigh piece spreads when you sit, a bicep piece shortens when the arm flexes. None of these are forbidden placements; all of them are placements where the check must happen in motion. Stencil on, then bend, twist, reach, grip, and sit — and watch what the artwork does. If it only works standing still, it does not work.</p>

<h2>Orientation: who is the design for?</h2>
<p>By long convention, text and faces are drawn to read correctly to the person looking at you, not to you looking down — the design's audience is the world, and a line of script that reads perfectly in your bathroom mirror reads upside down to everyone facing you. Plenty of wearers choose the opposite on purpose, for a piece that is private: a reminder written for its owner. Neither is wrong. What is wrong is discovering the choice after the fact, so settle it out loud at the stencil: who reads this, and from where? The same question applies to any design with a natural up — a bird, a ship, a face — on a limb that rotates. Decide which position of the arm is the design's home position, and align it there.</p>

<h2>Centered work and the symmetry tax</h2>
<p>A design on an arm can sit a quarter-inch off its ideal line and nobody will ever know. A centered design — chest, spine, throat, sternum — has no such mercy: slightly off-center there reads as a mistake, because the body itself supplies the ruler everyone measures it against. Centered work therefore costs more stencil time, not more money — marking the midline, checking from front and back, letting the client stand naturally rather than posed, since a design centered on a posed body is off-center on a relaxed one. If your piece is centered and your artist rushes this part, that is information. Bodies are also not symmetric, and an honest stencil session discovers where yours aren't — one shoulder higher, a spine that curves gently — and places the design true to the body you have, not the diagram.</p>

<h2>Existing skin: scars, stretch marks, and the pieces already there</h2>
<p>The stencil goes onto the skin you actually have. Ink sits differently in scar tissue — it can take heavier or refuse in patches — and a scar should be fully mature, usually a year or more, before it is tattooed over; your artist will want to see and feel it before promising a result. Stretch marks behave similarly: the texture shows through anything tattooed across them, which for some clients is the point and for others is a reason to shift the design a few inches. And most stencil sessions are not working on blank skin. A new design near old ones gets checked against its neighbors — spacing, flow, whether the styles argue — because pieces placed one at a time still end up living together. Tell your artist where your plans might grow; a design placed with its future neighbors in mind leaves them somewhere good to go.</p>

<h2>The mirror check: ten free minutes, used properly</h2>
<p>Everything above converges here. The stencil is on, the needle is not yet out, and this is the last moment where every change is free. Run the check in full:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Stand naturally.</strong> Not posed, not sucked in, not twisted toward the mirror — the body the tattoo will actually live on.</li>
<li><strong>Look from distance first, then close.</strong> Across the room it should read as one clear subject; up close is for the details.</li>
<li><strong>Move.</strong> Bend the joint, twist the limb, sit, reach. Watch the design do what it will do ten thousand times.</li>
<li><strong>Read the lettering out loud, letter by letter.</strong> If it is text, spell it. A misspelling caught at the stencil costs nothing; caught at home, it costs everything. Check names and dates twice — they are the text nobody proofreads because they feel too familiar to be wrong.</li>
<li><strong>Check it in the mirror and directly.</strong> The mirror shows you what the world sees; direct view shows you what you will see. Some placements — anything you will mostly view in mirrors — deserve the mirror's verdict weighted heavier.</li>
<li><strong>Say the doubt out loud.</strong> "A little bigger," "two inches lower," "I am not sure about the tilt" — said now, these are two-minute fixes. Artists re-stencil all day and would rather move a design three times than tattoo a placement you were politely unsure about. Silence in this ten minutes is the only real mistake available to you.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The short version</h2>
<p>Size is a number, settled in inches against floors that exist for physical reasons. Placement is fitted to the body's architecture, then tested in motion, because skin moves. Orientation is a decision — who reads the design, and from where. Centered work pays a symmetry tax in stencil time. And the mirror check is ten free minutes that catch, in order: wrong size, wrong spot, wrong direction, and misspelled names — the four horsemen of tattoo regret, all of them harmless while they are still purple lines on skin. Bring the design; the stencil is where it becomes yours. Browse the <a href="/gallery">gallery</a> for the design itself, or have one <a href="/orders/custom">drawn to your measurements</a>.</p>

